From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 17:24:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] clutter/color: Adapt a display's output to a white point

Night Light shifts the white point of an output. Its only
implementation scales the encoded values in the CRTC gamma LUT, which
for a PQ encoding is neither a white point shift nor a uniform dimming.
The white point has to be adapted in linear light instead, as part of
the output's color conversion.

Let a display target carry a white point its output is adapted to, made
with clutter_color_state_params_new_adapted_display_target() and kept
by the blending states derived from it. It is applied once, in linear
BT.2020 just before the conversion from a state that carries it to one
that does not: the conversion of the blending state to the output's
encoding. Content converted to the blending state is not adapted.

The adaptation is made in the frame of the colors the display shows:
linear BT.2020 is taken to CIE XYZ with the encoding's white point,
adapted with Bradford from that white point to the new one, and brought
back, so the encoding's white is shown at the new white point. It is
scaled so that the brightest channel of the adapted white, in linear
RGB of the encoding, stays that of the unadapted white: the output
never becomes brighter.

The adaptation is a matrix uniform of the conversion, so a new white
point keeps its shader. The white point is compared exactly in the
identity of a color state, so a changed value makes a new state and a
new transform. Direct scanout and the cursor plane bypass the
conversion, so clutter_color_transform_requires_composition() tells
when they cannot show content as the renderer would.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DLZiLBYev72J1s4D37L7Bi
